Spec: specs/002-capture-and-canonical-events/spec.md (Principal seam, Tee, Exchange record, Memory bound, Redaction, Logs; AC8, AC22, AC24, AC29, supports AC7, AC12)
Why this approach: capture hangs off the three hooks 001 already owns (ServeHTTP wraps the writer, Rewrite wraps the body as watcher(tee(body)) and snapshots the upstream headers, Settle finishes the exchange), so nothing new sits on the byte path. The adapter's SecretDeclarer and ParsingAdapter are read once per proxy. NewProxy keeps 001's signature with capture off; the registry passes WithCapture's Capture to an unexported constructor, so every 001 caller is unchanged.
Alternatives rejected: a variadic option on NewProxy (not in the plan's interfaces; tests reach the capturing constructor through export_test.go instead); an always-present capture field on proxied lines driven by Protocol (it would break 001's exact-key access-log tests for handlers that set Protocol without a proxy, so the field follows Meta.Capture instead); taking the exchange path from the upstream URL (it carries base_url's path).
Trade-off / known limit: a dropped_memory exchange is logged on the access line but not yet counted anywhere; the sink's counters (T13) or the shutdown line (T16) need to take it. The response header snapshot now leaves out value-less entries (the anti-sniff Content-Type placeholder), since they are never sent. Recorded in plan.md ("Details fixed in T8").
Verified by: make verify; go test -race -count=20 over TestCapture_MemoryLimitDrops (over_limit, crosses_limit_mid_stream), TestRedact_AdapterQueryParams, TestCapture_ExchangeFields (non_streamed, streamed, request_incomplete), TestCapture_OutgoingRequestHasNoGetBody/through_proxy, TestAccessLog_CaptureField; tests first failed to build on the missing Exchange, WithCapture and export doors; request_incomplete and the GetBody guard each proved by breaking the code for a moment (forcing RequestIncomplete false, setting GetBody in Rewrite) and seeing them fail, then reverted. 001's tests pass unchanged.
